Looking at the code fragments and the Hexi library usage patterns, I can implement the two functions. Based on the examples, I can see how to use the binary_stream with buffer adaptors, read operations, and various stream methods.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/hexi.h>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>

struct NetworkPacket {
    uint32_t packet_id;
    std::string username;
    std::vector<uint32_t> scores;
    std::string metadata;
};

NetworkPacket deserialize_packet(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor for the input span
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    NetworkPacket packet;

    // Read the packet_id
    stream.read(packet.packet_id);

    // Read the username using the length prefix
    stream.read_prefixed(packet.username);

    // Read the score count, then use read_container to read the scores vector
    stream.read_container(packet.scores);

    // Read the metadata string as null-terminated for zero-copy
    stream.read_null_terminated(packet.metadata);

    // Skip any remaining padding bytes (if any)
    // The stream automatically handles position, so remaining bytes are implicitly skipped

    return packet;
}

struct PacketInfo {
    uint32_t packet_id;
    std::string_view username;
};

PacketInfo extract_packet_info(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor for the input span
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    PacketInfo info;

    // Use span() to get a zero-copy view of the packet_id (first 4 bytes)
    auto packet_id_span = stream.span(sizeof(uint32_t));
    std::memcpy(&info.packet_id, packet_id_span.data(), sizeof(uint32_t));

    // Read username using prefixed adaptor to get a string_view
    info.username = stream.read_prefixed<std::string_view>();

    // Skip the scores vector without reading its elements
    // Read count first
    uint32_t score_count;
    stream.read(score_count);

    // Skip the data (score_count * sizeof(uint32_t) bytes)
    stream.skip(score_count * sizeof(uint32_t));

    return info;
}
```

This implementation:

1. **`deserialize_packet`**:
   - Creates a buffer adaptor and binary stream from the input span
   - Reads `packet_id` directly using `read()`
   - Reads `username` with length prefix using `read_prefixed()`
   - Reads the scores vector using `read_container()` which handles the count and elements
   - Reads `metadata` as a null-terminated string using `read_null_terminated()`

2. **`extract_packet_info`**:
   - Uses `span()` to get a zero-copy view of the first 4 bytes for packet_id
   - Uses `read_prefixed<std::string_view>()` to get a zero-copy view of the username
   - Reads the score count and then uses `skip()` to bypass the actual score data without deserializing it

