Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::file_buffer` is a file buffer class that wraps a `FILE*` handle and tracks read/write positions. Based on the test examples and the class structure, I need to:

1. Open the file using `hexi::file_buffer`
2. Read the header data
3. Restore the read position to preview without advancing the pointer

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/file_buffer.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <filesystem>

std::vector<uint8_t> preview_file_header(const std::filesystem::path& file_path, size_t header_size) {
    // Open the file using hexi::file_buffer
    hexi::file_buffer buffer(file_path);

    // Check if file opened successfully
    if (!buffer) {
        return {}; // Return empty vector on error
    }

    // Get current read position (should be 0 at start)
    auto current_pos = buffer.tell_read();

    // Prepare destination buffer
    std::vector<uint8_t> header_data(header_size);

    // Read the header data
    auto bytes_read = buffer.read(header_data.data(), header_size);

    // Check if read was successful
    if (!buffer || bytes_read != header_size) {
        // If we couldn't read the full header, resize to actual bytes read
        header_data.resize(bytes_read);
    }

    // Restore the read position to preview without advancing
    buffer.seek_read(current_pos);

    return header_data;
}
```

