Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides `serialise()` and `deserialise()` methods on the stream objects that work with custom objects that have a `serialise()` method. Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& stream) {
        stream << user_id << name;
    }
};

void serialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const UserData& data) {
    // Use the writer's serialise method which calls the object's serialise() method
    writer.serialise(data);
}

void deserialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader, UserData& data) {
    // Use the reader's deserialise method which calls the object's serialise() method
    reader.deserialise(data);
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`serialize_object`**: Uses `writer.serialise(data)` which internally calls the `UserData::serialise()` method with the writer stream. The object's `serialise()` method then writes `user_id` and `name` to the stream using the `<<` operator.

2. **`deserialize_object`**: Uses `reader.deserialise(data)` which internally calls the `UserData::serialise()` method with the reader stream. Since the `serialise()` method uses the `<<` operator which works bidirectionally with Hexi streams (based on the stream type), it will read the data when used with a reader.

The key insight from the code fragments is that Hexi uses a unified `serialise()` method for both serialization and deserialization, where the stream adaptor determines whether data is being written or read based on the stream type (writer vs reader).

