Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library supports reading and writing strings with a prefixed format (length-prefixed strings). From the test code, I can see that:

1. The default behavior for strings uses a prefixed format (length prefix)
2. The library uses the `<<` operator for writing and `>>` operator for reading
3. There's a `hexi::prefixed()` adaptor that can be used explicitly for prefixed strings

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <string>

void write_string_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::string& str) {
    // Write the string using the default prefixed format
    // The << operator automatically writes the length prefix followed by the string data
    writer << str;
}

std::string read_string_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read the string using the default prefixed format
    // The >> operator automatically reads the length prefix and then the string data
    std::string result;
    reader >> result;
    return result;
}
```

The implementation is straightforward because the Hexi library's `binary_stream_writer` and `binary_stream_reader` classes already have built-in support for reading and writing length-prefixed strings through their overloaded `<<` and `>>` operators. 

When you write a string using `writer << str`, it automatically:
1. Writes the length of the string as a prefix
2. Writes the string data

When you read a string using `reader >> str`, it automatically:
1. Reads the length prefix
2. Reads that many bytes as the string data

This matches the test case `binary_stream_pmc.string_adaptor_default` which shows that the default behavior for strings is to use the prefixed format.

