Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides `binary_stream_writer` and `binary_stream_reader` classes with methods to write and read data. Based on the test code showing container operations, I can see that the library supports writing containers with the `<<` operator and reading with `>>` operator or `get<T>()` method.

For writing a container with a prefixed count, I need to:
1. Write the count (size) of the container first
2. Write each element

For reading:
1. Read the count first
2. Read that many elements

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void write_container_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::vector<uint32_t>& container) {
    // Write the count first (as uint32_t to match the container element type)
    uint32_t count = static_cast<uint32_t>(container.size());
    writer.put(count);

    // Write each element
    for (const auto& element : container) {
        writer.put(element);
    }
}

std::vector<uint32_t> read_container_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read the count first
    uint32_t count = reader.get<uint32_t>();

    // Create vector and reserve space
    std::vector<uint32_t> container;
    container.reserve(count);

    // Read each element
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        container.push_back(reader.get<uint32_t>());
    }

    return container;
}
```

This implementation:
- Uses `writer.put()` to write individual values to the stream
- Uses `reader.get<T>()` to read individual values from the stream
- Writes the container size as a prefix so the reader knows how many elements to read
- Reserves space in the vector for efficiency when reading
- Follows the pattern shown in the test code fragments where primitives are written and read individually

