Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

bool check_can_write_seek(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    // Check if the buffer supports write seeking by calling can_write_seek()
    return buffer.can_write_seek();
}

void seek_write_position(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, hexi::buffer_seek direction, std::size_t offset) {
    // Seek the write position using write_seek() with the specified direction and offset
    buffer.write_seek(direction, offset);
}

void update_header_at_position(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, uint32_t header_value) {
    // Save current write position
    std::size_t header_position = buffer.write_ptr();

    // Write a placeholder header (4 bytes for uint32_t)
    uint32_t placeholder = 0;
    buffer.write(reinterpret_cast<const std::byte*>(&placeholder), sizeof(uint32_t));

    // Write some body data (example: just to demonstrate the concept)
    // In a real scenario, you would write actual body content here
    std::size_t body_position = buffer.write_ptr();

    // Now seek back to the header position to update it
    buffer.write_seek(hexi::buffer_seek::absolute, header_position);

    // Write the actual header value
    buffer.write(reinterpret_cast<const std::byte*>(&header_value), sizeof(uint32_t));

    // Seek back to the end (after the header) to continue writing if needed
    buffer.write_seek(hexi::buffer_seek::absolute, body_position);
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`check_can_write_seek`**: Calls the `can_write_seek()` method on the buffer adaptor to check if write seeking is supported.

2. **`seek_write_position`**: Uses the `write_seek()` method with the provided `buffer_seek` direction enum (which can be `forward`, `backward`, or `absolute`) and offset to move the write position.

3. **`update_header_at_position`**: 
   - Saves the current write position (where the header will be)
   - Writes a placeholder header value
   - Records the position after the header (for body content)
   - Seeks back to the header position using `buffer_seek::absolute`
   - Writes the actual header value
   - Seeks back to continue writing after the header

This implementation follows the pattern of the Hexi library's buffer management system and demonstrates proper use of write seeking functionality.

