Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `buffer_adaptor` has two methods:
1. `clear()` - which resets both read and write cursors
2. `conditional_clear()` - which clears the buffer only when read and write pointers are at the same position

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void clear_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    // Clear the buffer and reset both read and write cursors
    buffer.clear();
}

void conditional_clear_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    // Conditionally clear the buffer when read and write pointers are at the same position
    buffer.conditional_clear();
}
```

The implementation is straightforward:

1. **`clear_buffer`**: Calls the `clear()` method on the buffer adaptor, which resets both the read and write cursors back to the beginning of the buffer.

2. **`conditional_clear_buffer`**: Calls the `conditional_clear()` method on the buffer adaptor, which checks if the read pointer equals the write pointer (meaning all data has been read), and only then calls `clear()` to reset the cursors.

