Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides binary stream reading/writing capabilities and has support for varint encoding (as evidenced by `hexi::prefixed_varint` in the test files). Let me implement the three functions based on the standard varint encoding scheme:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<typename T>
std::size_t encode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, T value) {
    // Convert to unsigned type for proper bit manipulation
    using unsigned_type = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    unsigned_type uvalue = static_cast<unsigned_type>(value);

    std::size_t bytes_written = 0;

    // Encode using varint format: 7 bits of data per byte, MSB indicates continuation
    while (uvalue >= 0x80) {
        // Write lower 7 bits with continuation bit set
        std::uint8_t byte = static_cast<std::uint8_t>((uvalue & 0x7F) | 0x80);
        writer << byte;
        uvalue >>= 7;
        bytes_written++;
    }

    // Write final byte (no continuation bit)
    std::uint8_t byte = static_cast<std::uint8_t>(uvalue & 0x7F);
    writer << byte;
    bytes_written++;

    return bytes_written;
}

template<typename T>
T decode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    using unsigned_type = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    unsigned_type result = 0;
    int shift = 0;

    // Decode varint format
    while (true) {
        std::uint8_t byte;
        reader >> byte;

        // Add the lower 7 bits to result
        result |= static_cast<unsigned_type>(byte & 0x7F) << shift;

        // If MSB is not set, this is the last byte
        if ((byte & 0x80) == 0) {
            break;
        }

        shift += 7;

        // Prevent overflow for large values
        if (shift >= sizeof(T) * 8) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return static_cast<T>(result);
}

template<typename T>
bool encode_decode_roundtrip(T original_value) {
    // Create a buffer and stream for encoding
    std::vector<char> buffer;
    hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer writer(adaptor);

    // Encode the value
    encode_varint(writer, original_value);

    // Create a reader for decoding
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader reader(adaptor);

    // Decode the value
    T decoded_value = decode_varint<T>(reader);

    // Compare with original
    return decoded_value == original_value;
}
```

This implementation:

1. **`encode_varint`**: Encodes an integer using the standard varint format where each byte uses 7 bits for data and the MSB (most significant bit) as a continuation flag. If the MSB is 1, more bytes follow; if 0, it's the last byte.

2. **`decode_varint`**: Reads bytes from the stream and reconstructs the integer by combining the 7-bit chunks, stopping when it encounters a byte without the continuation bit set.

3. **`encode_decode_roundtrip`**: Creates a buffer and stream, encodes the value, then decodes it back and verifies that the decoded value matches the original.

The implementation handles both signed and unsigned integer types by converting to unsigned for bit manipulation, which is the standard approach for varint encoding.

